course: dictionary
lesson: 11184
title: Wise words and wise choices
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Wise means having good sense and making good choices, because you have knowledge and experience. A wise person thinks before acting. «It would be wise to wear a helmet» means that wearing one is the sensible choice. Wise is an adjective, so it can change to wiser and wisest.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «Eyes and memory can trick us; being sure doesn't always mean being right, so it is wise to check before telling others.» Checking before you speak is a sensible choice, so wise means sensible here. Test it: put sensible in place of wise, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Wise is not only about knowing lots of facts. In old stories the wise owl gives good advice, because wisdom shows in the choices a person makes. In Kipling's tale, the wise person stops a boast without shaming the boaster: «wise people can stop a boast without shaming the boaster.»
explain: «Wise to» is an informal phrase. If you are wise to a trick, you know about it and cannot be fooled. Some people say «none the wiser» when they still do not understand something after being told about it.
fact: Wise means having good sense and making good choices.
fact: A wise decision or action is a sensible one.
fact: «Wise» can change to wiser and wisest.
fact: «Wise to» (informal) means knowing about a trick, so you cannot be fooled by it.
fact: «None the wiser» means still not knowing something, even after you have been told.
exercise: «It would be wise to wear a helmet when you cycle.» What does wise mean here? | *Sensible | Very loud | Silly | Tired
exercise: «The old king gave wise words to his children.» What are wise words? | *Words that show good sense | Words in a foreign language | Words said very quickly | Words that rhyme
exercise: «Eyes and memory can trick us, so it is wise to check before telling others.» Why is checking wise? | Because checking is always fun | Because it takes a long time | Because it makes others cross | *Because it is the sensible choice
test: «Sam was wise to the trick, so the magician could not fool him.» What does wise to mean here? | *Knowing about a trick | Asleep during the show | Liking magic shows | Afraid of the magician
test: Which word is the comparative form of wise, meaning more wise? | Wisdom | *Wiser | Wised | Wisely
exam: In the old owl stories, what makes the owl wise? | *Good advice and sensible choices, not just facts | Living in a palace | Having the biggest eyes | Flying only at night
